% Required packages (optional, but useful for formatting)
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{fancyhdr}
\usepackage{titlesec}

% Set up the document
\title{Automatically Generated Table of Contents Example}
\author{Your Name}
\date{\today}

% Customize section formatting (optional)
\titleformat{\section}{\large\bfseries\uppercase}{}{0em}{}
\titleformat{\subsection}{\normalsize\bfseries}{}{0em}{}

% Set up page headers (optional)
\pagestyle{fancy}
\fancyhf{}
\fancyhead[R]{\thepage}
\fancyfoot[C]{\footnotesize \textit{Generated with LaTeX}}

\begin{document}

% Title and author
\maketitle

% Table of Contents - this generates the TOC automatically
\tableofcontents
\clearpage

% Section and subsection content
\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section. It provides an overview of the document.

\section{Background}
The background section discusses the context and relevance of the topic.

\subsection{Historical Context}
This subsection explores the historical development of the subject.

\section{Methodology}
Here we describe the methods used in the study.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Details on how data was gathered.

\subsection{Analysis Techniques}
The tools and techniques applied to analyze the data.

\section{Results}
Presented are the findings from the analysis.

\subsection{Key Findings}
Summary of major outcomes.

\section{Conclusion}
This section concludes the document and suggests future work.

\end{document}
```

### Key Commands Explained:
- `\documentclass[12pt]{article}`: Defines the document type and font size.
- `\usepackage{...}`: Loads packages to enhance formatting (e.g., `fancyhdr` for headers).
- `\title`, `\author`, `\date`: Set metadata for the document.
- `\maketitle`: Generates the title page.
- `\tableofcontents`: Automatically generates the table of contents based on all sections and subsections defined in the document.
- `\clearpage`: Ensures the TOC appears on a new page (recommended practice).

### How It Works:
When you compile this LaTeX file (using `pdflatex`, `xelatex`, or `lualatex`), the `\tableofcontents` command scans all `\section`, `\subsection`, and similar commands and generates a formatted list of contents that appears at the beginning of the document.

